# Equitable Genomic Medicine: The Equity Feasibility Region as a Deployment-Admissibility Criterion


ouadi maakoul


## Abstract

Polygenic scores and genomic risk prediction models are increasingly proposed for clinical deployment,
yet their performance varies systematically across ancestral populations and social contexts. The 2026 
evidence-standards literature has advanced a benchmarking paradigm spanning discrimination, calibration, 
equity gaps, and computational cost, together with living, auditable frameworks for multi-ancestry prediction.
This paper argues that benchmarking and admissibility are distinct epistemic operations. Benchmarking measures;
admissibility constrains. We propose the Equity Feasibility Region \(\mathcal{V}_E\) as a formal decision-theoretic 
object that transforms equity from a post hoc evaluation statistic into a pre-specified condition governing
progression from genomic inference to clinical and economic deployment. We provide operational definitions for each
dimension of \(\mathcal{V}_E\), correct several technical errors in prior formulations—including the harmonic mean 
p-value calibration, DeLong variance bias, local ancestry tract-structure failure, and the population-conditional 
QC threshold—and extend the framework to a dynamic viability formulation \(\theta_t \in \mathcal{V}_E(t)\) in which 
the feasibility region evolves as new evidence accumulates. The economic and incentive layers are positioned as 
downstream applications of the admissibility criterion rather than as independent theoretical contributions.

