## Appendix F: The Equity Feasibility Region — Formal Properties

### F.1 Definition and Basic Properties

**Definition F.1 (Equity Feasibility Region).** Let \(\Theta\) be the parameter space of genomic prediction models. Let \(\Delta_r: \Theta \to \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}\) for \(r = 1, \ldots, R\) be equity dimension functions. Let \(\boldsymbol{\delta} = (\delta_1, \ldots, \delta_R) \in \mathbb{R}_{\geq 0}^R\) be pre-specified tolerances. The Equity Feasibility Region is
\[
\mathcal{V}_E = \left\{ \theta \in \Theta : \bigwedge_{r=1}^{R} \Delta_r(\theta) \leq \delta_r \right\}.
\]

**Proposition F.2 (Monotonicity in Tolerances).** If \(\boldsymbol{\delta} \leq \boldsymbol{\delta}'\) componentwise, then \(\mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}) \subseteq \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}')\).

*Proof.* Let \(\theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta})\). Then \(\Delta_r(\theta) \leq \delta_r \leq \delta_r'\) for all \(r\). Hence \(\theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}')\). \(\square\)

**Proposition F.3 (Intersection Structure).** Define \(\mathcal{V}_E^{(r)} = \{\theta : \Delta_r(\theta) \leq \delta_r\}\). Then
\[
\mathcal{V}_E = \bigcap_{r=1}^{R} \mathcal{V}_E^{(r)}.
\]

*Proof.* Immediate from the definition. \(\square\)

### F.2 Operational Definitions of Equity Dimensions

| Dimension | Metric | Constraint |
|---|---|---|
| Discrimination | \(\Delta_1(\theta) = \max_{A,B} \|\text{AUC}_A - \text{AUC}_B\|\) | \(\leq \delta_1\) |
| Calibration | \(\Delta_2(\theta) = \max_{A,B} \|\text{CalErr}_A - \text{CalErr}_B\|\) | \(\leq \delta_2\) |
| FDR | \(\Delta_3(\theta) = \max_{A,B} \|\text{FDR}_A - \text{FDR}_B\|\) | \(\leq \delta_3\) |
| Access | \(\Delta_4(\theta) = \max_{A,B} \|\text{Coverage}_A - \text{Coverage}_B\|\) | \(\leq \delta_4\) |
| Utility | \(\Delta_5(\theta) = \max_{A,B} \|\text{NetBenefit}_A - \text{NetBenefit}_B\|\) | \(\leq \delta_5\) |

### F.3 The Benchmarking–Admissibility Distinction

**Definition F.4 (Benchmarking Operator).** A benchmarking operator \(\mathcal{B}\) maps a model \(\theta\) and data \(D\) to a vector of measurements:
\[
\mathcal{B}(\theta, D) = (\Delta_1(\theta, D), \ldots, \Delta_R(\theta, D)).
\]

**Definition F.5 (Admissibility Operator).** An admissibility operator \(\mathcal{A}\) maps a model \(\theta\), data \(D\), and tolerances \(\boldsymbol{\delta}\) to a binary decision:
\[
\mathcal{A}(\theta, D, \boldsymbol{\delta}) =
\begin{cases}
1 & \text{if } \theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(\boldsymbol{\delta}) \\
0 & \text{otherwise.}
\end{cases}
\]

**Proposition F.6 (Non-Equivalence).** Benchmarking and admissibility are not equivalent operations. There exists \(\theta, D, \boldsymbol{\delta}\) such that \(\mathcal{B}(\theta, D)\) is defined but \(\mathcal{A}(\theta, D, \boldsymbol{\delta})\) is undefined (if \(\boldsymbol{\delta}\) is not pre-specified), and conversely, \(\mathcal{A}\) may be defined even when \(\mathcal{B}\) produces measurements that fail some benchmark thresholds if those thresholds were not pre-specified as admissibility conditions.

*Proof.* Construct \(\boldsymbol{\delta}\) post hoc from \(\mathcal{B}(\theta, D)\). Then \(\mathcal{A}\) returns 1 trivially, but the pre-specification requirement is violated. Conversely, if \(\boldsymbol{\delta}\) is pre-specified and \(\mathcal{B}(\theta, D)\) fails a non-admissibility benchmark, \(\mathcal{A}\) may still return 1. \(\square\)

