## Appendix G: Dynamic Viability of the Feasibility Region

### G.1 Dynamic Formulation

Let \(D_t\) denote the evidence base at time \(t\). Define
\[
\mathcal{V}_E(t) = \left\{ \theta : \Delta_r(\theta, D_t) \leq \delta_r, \; r = 1, \ldots, R \right\}.
\]
A deployed model \(\theta_t\) is dynamically admissible at time \(t\) if
\[
\theta_t \in \mathcal{V}_E(t).
\]

### G.2 Update Operator

When new evidence \(D_{\text{new}}\) arrives, the evidence base updates:
\[
D_{t+1} = D_t \cup D_{\text{new}},
\]
and the feasibility region evolves:
\[
\mathcal{V}_E(t+1) = \mathcal{F}\big(\mathcal{V}_E(t), D_{\text{new}}\big),
\]
where \(\mathcal{F}\) is the update operator.

**Proposition G.1 (Monotonicity of Evidence).** If \(D_{\text{new}}\) reduces the estimated disparity \(\Delta_r(\theta)\) for all \(\theta \in \Theta\), then \(\mathcal{V}_E(t) \subseteq \mathcal{V}_E(t+1)\).

*Proof.* If \(\Delta_r(\theta, D_{t+1}) \leq \Delta_r(\theta, D_t)\) for all \(\theta\), then \(\theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(t)\) implies \(\Delta_r(\theta, D_t) \leq \delta_r\), hence \(\Delta_r(\theta, D_{t+1}) \leq \delta_r\), so \(\theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(t+1)\). \(\square\)

**Corollary G.2 (Non-Monotonicity in General).** If new evidence increases the estimated disparity for some \(\theta\), then \(\mathcal{V}_E(t+1)\) may be strictly smaller than \(\mathcal{V}_E(t)\).

### G.3 Viability Condition

**Definition G.3 (Dynamic Viability).** A deployed genomic system is dynamically viable over the horizon \([0, T]\) if
\[
\theta_t \in \mathcal{V}_E(t)
\quad \text{for all } t \in [0, T].
\]

**Proposition G.4 (Viability as Trajectory Constraint).** Dynamic viability is a trajectory constraint, not a point constraint. A model that is admissible at \(t = 0\) may become inadmissible at \(t = T\) if the feasibility region contracts faster than the model can adapt.

