## Appendix M: Proofs of Main Results

### M.1 Proof of Proposition F.6 (Non-Equivalence)

**Proposition F.6.** Benchmarking and admissibility are not equivalent operations.

*Proof.* We construct a counterexample for each direction.

**(i) Benchmarking does not imply admissibility.** Let \(D\) be a dataset, \(\theta\) a model, and \(\mathcal{B}(\theta, D) = (\Delta_1, \ldots, \Delta_R)\) the benchmark measurements. Suppose no tolerances \(\boldsymbol{\delta}\) are pre-specified. Then \(\mathcal{A}(\theta, D, \boldsymbol{\delta})\) is undefined, even though \(\mathcal{B}(\theta, D)\) is well-defined. Hence benchmarking does not determine admissibility.

**(ii) Admissibility does not imply benchmarking passes.** Suppose \(\boldsymbol{\delta}\) is pre-specified. Let \(\mathcal{B}(\theta, D)\) include a non-admissibility benchmark \(B^*\) with threshold \(\delta^*\) that was not included in \(\boldsymbol{\delta}\). Then \(\mathcal{A}(\theta, D, \boldsymbol{\delta}) = 1\) even though \(\mathcal{B}(\theta, D)\) fails \(B^*\). Hence admissibility does not require passing all benchmarks.

Therefore benchmarking and admissibility are distinct operations. \(\square\)

### M.2 Proof of Proposition G.1 (Monotonicity of Evidence)

**Proposition G.1.** If \(D_{\text{new}}\) reduces the estimated disparity \(\Delta_r(\theta)\) for all \(\theta \in \Theta\), then \(\mathcal{V}_E(t) \subseteq \mathcal{V}_E(t+1)\).

*Proof.* Let \(\theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(t)\). Then \(\Delta_r(\theta, D_t) \leq \delta_r\) for all \(r\). By assumption, \(\Delta_r(\theta, D_{t+1}) \leq \Delta_r(\theta, D_t) \leq \delta_r\). Hence \(\theta \in \mathcal{V}_E(t+1)\). Since \(\theta\) was arbitrary, \(\mathcal{V}_E(t) \subseteq \mathcal{V}_E(t+1)\). \(\square\)

### M.3 Proof of Proposition D.3 (Family-Wise Equivalence)

**Proposition D.3.** Under the TOST procedure with Bonferroni correction at level \(\alpha/|\mathcal{P}|\), the family-wise error rate is controlled at level \(\alpha\).

*Proof.* For each pair \((i,j) \in \mathcal{P}\), the TOST procedure consists of two one-sided tests. Under the null \(H_{0,ij}: |\text{AUC}_i - \text{AUC}_j| \geq \delta\), the probability of rejecting \(H_{0,ij}\) is at most \(\alpha/|\mathcal{P}|\) (by the Bonferroni correction). By the union bound,
\[
P\left(\bigcup_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{P}} \{\text{Reject } H_{0,ij}\} \mid H_{0,ij} \text{ true}\right)
\leq
\sum_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{P}} \frac{\alpha}{|\mathcal{P}|}
=
\alpha.
\]
Hence the family-wise error rate is controlled at level \(\alpha\). \(\square\)

### M.4 Proof of Proposition E.1 (HMP Invariance)

**Proposition E.1.** The HMP is invariant to permutation of the p-values.

*Proof.* For any permutation \(\pi\) of \(\{1,\dots,k\}\),
\[
\text{HMP}(p_{\pi(1)}, \ldots, p_{\pi(k)})
=
\frac{k}{\sum_{a=1}^{k} \frac{1}{p_{\pi(a)}}}
=
\frac{k}{\sum_{a=1}^{k} \frac{1}{p_a}}
=
\text{HMP}(p_1, \ldots, p_k),
\]
where the second equality follows from the fact that summation is commutative. \(\square\)

### M.5 Proof of Proposition 3.1 (Population-Conditional Bayes QC Threshold)

**Proposition 3.1.** Under differentiability and an interior optimum, the population-specific threshold \(\tau_i^*\) satisfies
\[
\frac{f_i^1(\tau_i^*)}{f_i^0(\tau_i^*)} = \frac{c_\beta}{c_\alpha}.
\]

*Proof.* The first-order condition for the population-specific loss \(L_i(\tau) = c_\alpha F_i^1(\tau) + c_\beta (1 - F_i^0(\tau))\) is
\[
\frac{\partial L_i}{\partial \tau}
=
c_\alpha f_i^1(\tau) - c_\beta f_i^0(\tau) = 0.
\]
Rearranging,
\[
c_\alpha f_i^1(\tau_i^*) = c_\beta f_i^0(\tau_i^*).
\]
Dividing both sides by \(c_\alpha f_i^0(\tau_i^*)\),
\[
\frac{f_i^1(\tau_i^*)}{f_i^0(\tau_i^*)} = \frac{c_\beta}{c_\alpha}.
\]
The second-order condition requires that the derivative of the likelihood ratio is negative at \(\tau_i^*\), ensuring a minimum. \(\square\)

