## 3. Convergence and Complexity Analysis

### 3.1 Complexity of the Master Algorithm

| Phase | Operation | Complexity |
|---|---|---|
| I.1 | GRM construction | \(O(n^2 m)\) |
| I.2 | Eigendecomposition | \(O(n^3)\) |
| I.3 | Data rotation | \(O(n^2 m)\) |
| II | QC and threshold selection | \(O(nm + k \cdot K)\) |
| III | Score tests | \(O(nm)\) |
| IV | Stratified BH + HMP | \(O(m \log m)\) |
| V | AUC estimation | \(O(n^2)\) |
| VI | Equity dimensions | \(O(k^2)\) |
| VII | ICER curve | \(O(k)\) per \(\epsilon\) |
| VIII | Viability update | \(O(nm)\) |

**Total time complexity:**
\[
T(n,m,k) = O(n^3 + n^2 m + m \log m + k^2).
\]

**Space complexity:**
\[
S(n,m) = O(n^2 + nm).
\]

### 3.2 Convergence of the Bayes Threshold Solver

**Proposition 3.1 (Newton–Raphson Convergence).** Let \(\ell_a(\tau) = \log f_a^1(\tau) - \log f_a^0(\tau)\) be twice continuously differentiable. If \(\ell_a'(\tau_a^*) = \log(c_\beta/c_\alpha)\) and \(\ell_a''(\tau_a^*) < 0\), then the Newton–Raphson iteration converges quadratically to \(\tau_a^*\) in a neighborhood of the solution.

*Proof.* The iteration
\[
\tau_a^{(t+1)} = \tau_a^{(t)} - \frac{\ell_a'(\tau_a^{(t)}) - \log(c_\beta/c_\alpha)}{\ell_a''(\tau_a^{(t)})}
\]
is the standard Newton–Raphson scheme. Under the stated conditions, the convergence theorem for Newton–Raphson applies, yielding quadratic convergence. \(\square\)

### 3.3 Consistency of the Equity Verdict

**Proposition 3.2 (Verdict Consistency).** Under the assumptions that (i) the AUC estimators are consistent, (ii) the variance estimators are consistent, and (iii) the tolerance vector \(\boldsymbol{\delta}\) is pre-specified, the verdict \(\theta \in \mathcal{V}_E\) is consistent:
\[
P(\theta \in \mathcal{V}_E \mid \text{data}) \to \mathbf{1}\{\theta \in \mathcal{V}_E\} \quad \text{as } n \to \infty.
\]

*Proof.* By (i) and (ii), \(\Delta_r(\theta)\) and its standard error are consistently estimated. By (iii), the TOST procedure is consistent for the equivalence hypotheses. The family-wise equivalence test rejects all false null hypotheses with probability tending to 1, and the feasibility verdict follows. \(\square\)

